                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> Having looked at your comment, and it being the top search result, hopefully this will help other people who want to keep their theme values:\r\n\r\nTo be fair, default value [is already aligned with color scheme's colors](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c404fc5717866ff738aadeeb1f54ab8ed270ae1e/lua/mini/indentscope.lua#L701), as it links to built-in group. However, adjusting its color in a way that works with any color scheme indeed requires an autocommand for a `ColorScheme` event.\r\n\r\nOne more thing to add here is that I'd still suggest to use linking to some built-in highlight group, as it is a more robust solution. In this case, `vim.g.terminal_color_2` usually means green color. This is also the case for `DiagnosticOk` highlight group. So I'd use something like `vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'MiniIndentscopeSymbol', { link = 'DiagnosticOk' })`.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Having looked at your comment, and it being the top search result, hopefully this will help other people who want to keep their theme values:</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">To be fair, default value <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c404fc5717866ff738aadeeb1f54ab8ed270ae1e/lua/mini/indentscope.lua#L701\">is already aligned with color scheme's colors</a>, as it links to built-in group. However, adjusting its color in a way that works with any color scheme indeed requires an autocommand for a <code class=\"notranslate\">ColorScheme</code> event.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">One more thing to add here is that I'd still suggest to use linking to some built-in highlight group, as it is a more robust solution. In this case, <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.g.terminal_color_2</code> usually means green color. This is also the case for <code class=\"notranslate\">DiagnosticOk</code> highlight group. So I'd use something like <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'MiniIndentscopeSymbol', { link = 'DiagnosticOk' })</code>.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Having looked at your comment, and it being the top search result, hopefully this will help other people who want to keep their theme values:\n\nTo be fair, default value is already aligned with color scheme's colors, as it links to built-in group. However, adjusting its color in a way that works with any color scheme indeed requires an autocommand for a ColorScheme event.\nOne more thing to add here is that I'd still suggest to use linking to some built-in highlight group, as it is a more robust solution. In this case, vim.g.terminal_color_2 usually means green color. This is also the case for DiagnosticOk highlight group. So I'd use something like vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'MiniIndentscopeSymbol', { link = 'DiagnosticOk' }).",
